hey 42 here we humans have been going to  war for as long as we've existed and by  now we've gotten pretty good at it the  rules of engagement are fairly simple  one side starts a fight the other side  retaliates then the first side  retaliates to the retaliation and so on  and so forth until someone runs out of  land or bullets  or spears or whatever  take world war ii for example  on one side were the allies including  great britain the soviet union and the  united states  on the other side were the axis powers  including germany italy and japan  simple  or was it  because on the 5th of may 1945 just  three days before the fighting finally  came to an end in europe  one of the strangest battles of the  entire war took place  one in which america fought alongside  germany against well  germany  bizarre comical and deadly serious in  equal measure the battle for ita castle  was one of history's side shows to the  main event an episode mostly forgotten  when people tell stories about the  biggest war in history  but one that perfectly captures the  horror and absurdity of battle in one  neat little tail  like all good castles the one found in  the village of ita in austria was built  on a hill  constructed in the 13th century it was  originally a fortress serving as a base  for princes counts bishops you know the  type  before being badly damaged in 1526  during the german peasants war  the structure was eventually rebuilt in  the late 1880s but this time with a  distinctly non-military usage in mind it  first served as a home to a famous  pianist who knew there was so much cash  in tickling the ivories  before being converted into a hotel  around the turn of the 20th century  a few decades later it was rebranded  again  this time as an upper-class lock-up for  vip prisoners of war  in 1938 austria was officially declared  part of the third reich during what is  now known as the angelus  that made castle ita nazi property and  as the war developed the former hotel  became a sub-camp of the infamous dachau  concentration camp located about 120  kilometers away  being the rather fancy castle that it  was ita was turned into a special prison  that would house the so-called erin  haflinger or detainees of honor he were  basically prisoners of particular value  to the nazis for one reason or another  they included high-profile members of  german society that had fallen out of  the nazi party senior political figures  from enemy countries and even a few  international sports stars even though  very few of the evan haflinger actually  survived the war for the most part the  conditions they were kept in were  luxurious compared to the poor souls  living in the concentration camps  there were no prison uniforms and there  was no forced labor and they ate the  same food as the soldiers who watched  over them  some of these prisoners were even able  to live with their husbands or wives and  received rations equivalent to nine  times that of an ordinary german  [Music]  in the final days of the war which is  when our story begins  the 14 prisoners held at casalita were  mainly frenchmen his number included two  former prime ministers a couple of  generals a tennis champion and several  members of the french resistance  they were a pretty mixed bunch all  things considered  but they were unified by their unusual  predicament  you see by this point in the war it had  become clear that the nazis weren't  going to be winning  on the one hand that was pretty good  news  but in some ways it only made the  situation more dangerous for the  prisoners of ita castle the main camp at  dakao had recently been liberated by  american troops and the guards of ita  castle deserted not long afterwards that  meant our ragtag band of vip prisoners  were essentially free to leave but  doing so was incredibly risky the third  rank may have been collapsing in  spectacular fashion  but like an injured animal backed into a  corner the germans were becoming more  unpredictable with each passing day  the area around the castle was teeming  with ss forces with orders to kill any  prisoners they came across  and that meant that former inmates  now occupants of ita castle  were trapped  and it was only a matter of time before  the ss turned up  to try and finish them off  [Music]  with little of a choice the prisoners  prepared to defend themselves taking up  weapons that had been left behind by  their former jailers but it was clear  they were going to need backup if they  were to have any hope of surviving  a plan was hastily hatched and placed in  the hands of well  a handy man  zvonimir kuczewicz  a yugoslav resistance fighter who'd been  brought up to the castle to do some odd  jobs was sent out in search of  reinforcements  he made his way to innsbruck where he  located a division of u.s troops led by  major john kramers who ignoring orders  from his superiors immediately  dispatched a contingent of american  soldiers to ita castle but the group was  forced back due to heavy shelling and  never made it to the castle  when it became clear that help wasn't on  its way the prisoners at the castle sent  out a second man to go in search of help  czech chef and prison cook andreas  crobot  crobat made his way by bicycle to the  nearby town of virgo where he was able  to make contact with people claiming to  be part of the austrian resistance  i say claiming to be part of the  austrian resistance  because the first thing these men did  was deliver crobat directly into the  hands of major joseph gangle commander  of a unit of vermont soldiers  in other words one of hitler's henchmen  or should i say one of hitler's former  henchmen  you see gangl had defected taking 20 of  his former vermax soldiers with him to  join the austrian resistance these  ex-nazis had been busy defending the  people of virgo against their former  colleagues the ss who were arbitrarily  executing anyone they deemed to be an  enemy or a deserter which as it happened  was pretty much everyone  when gangl heard about the plight of the  prisoners trapped up at the castle he  immediately agreed to help with only 20  men gangl knew he couldn't hope to  defend it as prisoners from a full-on ss  assault so instead he used his  resistance connections to make contact  with a group of american soldiers he  knew was stationed in the nearby town of  kustin  there he met captain jack lee commander  of a tank battalion in the 12th armored  division who quickly agreed to join the  rescue mission  by the time the castle's new defenders  showed up for duty  they were a bit of a motley crew  captain lee's rescue party included just  14 men and a solitary tank  and gangl arrived with only 10 soldiers  having lost half of his men in a  skirmish with the ss along the way  we've all been given christmas or  birthday presents that spectacularly  failed to live up to our expectations  and  i can only assume castle ita's prisoners  were experiencing similar feelings of  gratitude mixed with grave  disappointment at the arrival of these  reinforcements  on the one hand it was great to have  some experienced soldiers to lead the  defense of the castle  on the other  24 men and a single tank  wasn't exactly an overwhelming force  and the direct assault on the castle  from a heavily armed ss division would  still be a huge problem which was a bit  unfortunate since that's exactly what  happened next  under the leadership of captain lee the  unlikely mismatch of both german and  american soldiers  lined up alongside castleto's political  prisoners in preparation for the  impending attack  they didn't have to wait long at dawn on  the 5th of may 1945  around 150 waffen-ss soldiers mounted a  vicious assault on ita castle  but although the ss significantly  outnumbered the defenders they hadn't  counted on meeting such a well-organized  and spirited resistance  the castle itself also offered the  defenders a huge tactical advantage  still the waffen-ss soldiers were a  fairly ruthless bunch and many of them  had had years of experience in the art  of war crucially they were also far  better arms than their enemy as the  fighting intensified the nazi soldiers  managed to blow up the defender's soul  tank with some well-placed artillery  fire they also successfully cut comms to  the castle  but not before major gangle managed to  place a call to the austrian resistance  in virgo requesting backup  it was to be gangle's final act of the  battle he was taken out soon after by an  enemy sniper while protecting former  french prime minister paul reinu  the battle raged on and soon enough the  german and u.s forces defending the  castle were running desperately low on  ammunition  but it was at this point that an  unlikely hero volunteered for active  duty  former tennis champion jean baratra  three-time winner of wimbledon and  apparently one brave son of a [ __ ]  dodging volleys of bullets and charging  the net of the ss soldiers the brave  volunteer somehow managed to escape the  castle siege on foot  and as luck would have it he soon  stumbled across the nearby u.s  142nd infantry  baratra quickly filled in the unit's  commanders on the grave situation up at  the castle before asking for a uniform  and joining the rescuers who immediately  left on a mission to reach hitter before  the defenders ran out of ammo  like gandalf at helm's deep the relief  force arrived just in time to break the  advancing ss forces about a hundred of  whom were taken captive  [Music]  from there it was all over pretty  quickly and within a matter of hours the  prisoners of ita castle were on their  way home  captain lee was honored with a  distinguished service cross for his  courageous efforts  and major gangl the only man to die  during the battle was celebrated as a  hero his sacrifice commemorated by a  monument and a street in virgo that  bears his name to this day  after returning to france some of the  liberated prisoners made a massive  impact on political policies that shaped  the country in its post-war years  so in some ways the battle for castle  ita was a history-defining moment but  even without those grand consequences  it was a peculiar story that's worth  remembering even if only for the courage  of the people who looked beyond national  flags and bound together to do what was  right  [Music]  thanks for watching
 

